VI. Analysis A. Whether Mr. Wu has properly named the Respondent? [34] As a preliminary matter, the Respondent’s Memorandum of Fact and Law indicates that the “Attorney General of Canada”, and not the CRA, should be named as the Respondent in this matter. I agree with the AGC that the CRA is not the proper Respondent as it is not directly affected by the order sought in this application for judicial review: Rule 303 of the Federal Courts Rules, SOR/98-106; Aryan at para 13. The proper responding party is the “Attorney General of Canada”, because Mr. Wu is challenging the Final Second Review Decision made by the Final Second Reviewer of the CRA on behalf of the Minister of Employment and Social Development: definition of “Minister” at section 2 of the CRB Act reproduced above.

[36] The AGC has identified documents forming parts of exhibits included in Mr. Wu’s affidavit that were not before the Final Second Reviewer who rendered the Final Second Review Decision:
Exhibit “D” – Copy of a receipt from N. Wu dated January 1, 2020 in the amount of $125 (Applicant’s Record [AR] at 26);
Exhibit “L” – Copy of a letter dated May 22, 2024 from A. Lau (AR at 50);
Exhibit “L” – Copy of a cheque dated May 10, 2019 from Richmond Community Benefit Program Centre made out to F.V. Wu in the amount of $240 (AR at 51);
Exhibit “M” – Letter from TD Bank enclosing statement of deposits from January 1, 2020 to April 1, 2020 (AR at 53);
Exhibit “N” – Copies of three receipts from N. Wu dated June 30, 2020, July 31, 2020 and August 31, 2020 each in the amount of $600 (AR at 54);
Exhibit “N” – Copy of a deposit receipt from TD Bank (AR at 55); and
Exhibit “O” – Copies of an e-transfer and correspondence record from A. Lau to Mr. Wu (AR at 57, 60-63).

[37] I agree with the AGC.

